Database Table Design
To store the hierarchical menu data, you can use a single self-referencing table. Top-level menu items will have a parent_id of NULL.
Table: menu_items
| Column | Data Type | Constraints | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| id | BIGINT | PRIMARY KEY, AUTO_INCREMENT | The unique identifier for the menu item. |
| title | VARCHAR(255) | NOT NULL | The display text for the menu item (e.g., "Dashboard"). |
| route | VARCHAR(255) | NOT NULL | The navigation route (e.g., "/dashboard"). |
| icon | VARCHAR(100) |  | The name of the icon to display. |
| component | VARCHAR(255) |  | The component to be rendered for this route. |
| parent_id | BIGINT | FOREIGN KEY (id) | The id of the parent menu item. NULL for top-level items. |
| position | INT | NOT NULL, DEFAULT 0 | The sort order of the item within its level. |
| is_active | BOOLEAN | NOT NULL, DEFAULT true | Status of the menu item. false will exclude it. |
SQL Schema
Here is the SQL statement to create the table:
CREATE TABLE menu_items (
    id BIGINT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
    title VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    route VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    icon VARCHAR(100),
    component VARCHAR(255),
    parent_id BIGINT,
    position INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    is_active BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT true,
    FOREIGN KEY (parent_id) REFERENCES menu_items(id)
);

Java Spring Boot Code
This code follows a standard layered architecture: Controller -> Service -> Repository -> Entity.
1. Entity: MenuItem.java
This class maps to the menu_items database table. Annotations are used to filter inactive items and sort children automatically.
import jakarta.persistence.*;
import org.hibernate.annotations.Where;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

@Entity
@Table(name = "menu_items")
public class MenuItem {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    private String title;
    private String route;
    private String icon;
    private String component;
    
    @Column(name = "is_active")
    private boolean isActive = true;
    
    private int position;

    @OneToMany(cascade = CascadeType.ALL, fetch = FetchType.EAGER)
    @JoinColumn(name = "parent_id")
    @Where(clause = "is_active = true") // Only fetch active children
    @OrderBy("position ASC") // Order children by position
    private List<MenuItem> children = new ArrayList<>();

    // Getters and Setters
}

